WAVE TWO (the ratchet). Common brief: /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/research/i1-intent/brief-common.md. Your wave one:
SEAT/own-wave1.md. Your peer's: SEAT/peer-wave1.md. Read all three, then the code and the carrier again where you split.

You agree: ONE I1 lane, staged red-first (plane and signed image; stand-down, effect classes, read-side winner;
claims, operator release, causal voids; the face-join summons move plus the CLI's per-invocation face; the read-only
daemon view; the rubric witness); the far agent's relay seat stays out, after its own research. No clock decides a
claim; the winner reads, never elects; duplicates stay visible; consume K17's frontier, never a rival board; avoid the
W14-W16, K17-R and W26-slice-5 files. Settle, each with a verdict CANON (quote carrier line), DERIVED (name the house law
and the existing code it follows, file:line) or OPERATOR (options + one lean); surface OPERATOR only where both canon and
derivation truly run out:

F1. THE SIGNED IMAGE. Does the existing signed-act canon (signed-act.ts, persona-act.ts canonical image, domain
separation) settle the entry envelope and its hash version, so I1 reuses it with a new domain? Measure, don't guess.
F2. ANSWER vs ASKS. Sol asks whether an `answer` may name the executor of an unpinned single-effect intent. Quote the
carrier ("pin executor or require human answer naming atom"). Does canon already bind the answer to an ask id?
F3. THE IRREVERSIBLE EFFECT FENCE (the sharpest joint). Sol: outcome stand-down cannot stop two partitioned hearths from
both re-keying one joinee in face-join. MEASURE face-join.ts / operator-daemon-behavior.ts: what does a second
concurrent grant do to the Keyhive group (a second key? an idempotent no-op keyed by joinee? a fork?). If the
effect itself already converges or refuses at the Keyhive door, the fence derives; if it double-acts, name the
fence the effect door must check (operation id, pinned hearth) and whether canon's "single-effect verbs pin an
executor" already rules it (a face-join summons pinned to one hearth).
F4. CAUSAL BOUNDS AND `every`. Quote the carrier's bound vocabulary and `every` membership. What roster edge does a
bound name (the fleet board's edge acts? K17 frontier heads?), and does canon or the fleet board's existing edge
acts settle it? A finite campaign over "every device" — canon says later joins count; is a bound already the tool?
F5. SUPERSEDE AUTHORITY. Carrier: "the issuer's or operator's later word". Who counts as "operator" for a face (the
persona root? any device atom of the face? the seat?). Measure how Wave C names authority over a face's acts.
F6. THE VIEW. Duplicates, void-after-act and unknown frontier: does house law (show conflicts, never hide; silent-zero)
settle the pane, or does the operator own a choice?
F7. THE FINAL KIT: the slices in order with files held, each red + control + known positive, the readiness of each
slice against the code you measured, and THE OPERATOR LIST (only the survivors of F1-F6).

